a579c15126cce.png)\n\nFirst, we notice that the graph consists of two parts: the line segment for $x \\in [-2,0]$ and the line segment for $x \\in [0,2].$ Therefore, using the additive property of the definite integral, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-2}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x = \\underbrace{\\int_{-2}^{0} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_1} + \\underbrace{\\int_{0}^{2} f(x)\\: \\textrm{d}x}_{A_2}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis can be represented using definite integrals. We divide the region between our curve and the $x$-axis as follows:\n\n![](images/3e/3eabe4f363f93b7e4d005c6c1f0beba8e9ff9e74ebae5410930565baa8569e41.png)\n\nThe first integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [-2,0].$ The corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe second integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x \\in [0,2].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}ab \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2} \\\\  & =3+2 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186102038 1064 "Suppose we want to find a solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\ny' = (1-2x)y^2 \\,\n$$\n\nsatisfying the additional condition $y(0)=1$. This additional condition $y(0)=1$ is called an **initial condition** or sometimes a **boundary condition** of the differential equation.\n\nFirst of all, we note that the given differential equation is separable, so we can separate variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (1 - 2x)y^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx} &= 1 - 2x \\\\\n\\int\\frac{1}{y^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{dy}{dx}dx &= \\int(1 - 2x)dx \\\\\n\\int\\frac{dy}{y^{2}} &= \\int(1 - 2x)dx \\\\\n- \\frac{1}{y} &= x - x^{2} + C \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} &= x^{2} - x - C \\\\\ny &= \\frac{1}{x^{2} - x - C}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac {1} {x^2 - x - C} \\,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nNow we choose the constant $C$ such that the condition $y(0)=1$ is satisfied. Substituting $x=0$ and $y=1$ into the general solution and solving for $C$, we get\n\n$$\ny(0) = \\frac{1}{0 - 0 - C} = 1⇒C =-1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution of\n\n$$\ny' = (1-2x)y^2\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the condition $y(0)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac {1} {x^2 - x - (-1)} = \\dfrac {1} {x^2 - x + 1}\\,\n$$\n\nTo recap: the solution of a first-order separable ordinary differential equation (ODE) with an initial condition is obtained by\n\n1. separating the variables,\n2. integrating the equation, and then\n3. substituting the condition into the solution to solve for the constant of integration." 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186102038 1064 "Suppose we want to find a solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\ny' = (1-2x)y^2 \\,,\n$$\n\nsatisfying the additional condition $y(0)=1.$ This additional condition $y(0)=1$ is called an **initial condition** or sometimes a **boundary condition** of the differential equation. \n\nFirst of all, we note that the given differential equation is separable, so we can separate variables and integrate both sides with respect to $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(1-2x){y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1-2x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{{y}^{2}}\\, & =\\int (1-2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\frac{1}{y} & =x-{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & ={x}^{2}-x-C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-x-C}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny    =   \\dfrac {1} {x^2 - x - C} \\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant. \n\nNow we choose the constant $C$ such that the condition $y(0)=1$ is satisfied. Substituting $x=0$ and $y=1$ into the general solution and solving for $C,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0)=\\frac{1}{0-0-C}=1\\,\\Rightarrow \\,C=-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution of $y' = (1-2x)y^2$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=1$ is\n\n$$\ny    =   \\dfrac {1} {x^2 - x - (-1)} = \\dfrac {1} {x^2 - x + 1}\\,.\n$$\n\nTo recap: the solution of a first-order separable ordinary differential equation (ODE) with an initial condition is obtained by \n\n- separating the variables,\n\n- integrating the equation, and then\n\n- substituting the condition into the solution to solve for the constant of integration." 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186102040 1064 "The region $\\textrm{R}$, shown below is the area under the graph of\n\n$$\nf(x) = 2x+1\n$$\n\nover the interval $[1,3]$. What is the height of the rectangle defined over the same interval that has the same area as $\\textrm{R}$?\n\n![](images/d8/d84e22705906cc37909281a2411d70494946da5541f1c5e56dd278e10404533e.png)\n\nUsing the formula for the area of a trapezoid, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\textrm{Area}\\,(\\textrm{R}) = \\dfrac{1}{2}(3+7)(3-1)= 10. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the rectangle to have the same area, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n10 &= f(c)(3 - 1) \\\\\nf(c) &= 5 \\\\\n2c + 1 &= 5 \\\\\nc &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can summarize what we've just done by saying that\n\n$$\n\\int_1^3 (2x +1)\\, \\textrm d x= f(2) \\cdot (3-1)\n$$\n\nThis is an example of the **mean value theorem for integrals**, which states:\n\n> *If $f(x)$ is a continuous function on the closed interval $[a, b]$, then there exists a number $c$ in that interval such that*\n> $\\displaystyle \\int_a^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x = f(c) \\cdot (b-a)$." 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186102040 1064 "The region $\\textrm{R},$ shown below, is the area under the graph of $f(x) = 2x+1$ over the interval $[1,3].$ What is the height of the rectangle defined over the same interval that has the same area as $\\textrm{R}?$ \n\n![](images/01/01597200b1f0e147fa8c53b6301d0c8e37208b5a6cce64cf0d9859d1220d8385.png)\n\nUsing the formula for the area of a trapezoid, we have that\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\textrm{Area}\\,(\\textrm{R}) = \\dfrac{1}{2}(3+7)(3-1)= 10.}\n$$\n\nFor the rectangle to have the same area, we must have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =f(c)(3-1) \\\\ f(c) & =5 \\\\ 2c+1 & =5 \\\\ c & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can summarize what we've just done by saying that\n\n$$\n\\int_1^3 (2x +1)\\, \\textrm d x= f(2) \\cdot (3-1).\n$$\n\nThis is an example of the **mean value theorem for integrals**, which states:\n\n> *If $f(x)$ is a continuous function on the closed interval $[a, b]$, then there exists a number $c$ in that interval such that*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\int_a^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x = f(c) \\cdot (b-a).\n> $$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186102041 1064 "The **average value** of a continuous function on a closed interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_\\textrm{avg} = \\dfrac{1}{b-a}\\int_a^b f(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186102041 1064 "The **average value** of a continuous function on a closed interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_\\textrm{avg} = \\dfrac{1}{b-a}\\int_a^b f(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186107071 1064 "Consider the graph of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ below. How can we determine the intervals where $f'(x)$ is positive or negative?\n\nThe first step is to identify the points where the derivative is zero. Here, the critical points are $x=2$ (a local maximum) and $x=4$ (a local minimum), and $f'(x) = 0$ at these points.\n\n![](images/de/de01a8