true? To understand where this result comes from, first notice that in the formula for the triple product, the cross product can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nb \\cdot c &= \\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ b_{1} & b_{2} & b_{3} \\\\ c_{1} & c_{2} & c_{3}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}b_{2} & b_{3} \\\\ c_{2} & c_{3}\\end{vmatrix}i -\\begin{vmatrix}b_{1} & b_{3} \\\\ c_{1} & c_{3}\\end{vmatrix}j +\\begin{vmatrix}b_{1} & b_{2} \\\\ c_{1} & c_{2}\\end{vmatrix}k\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we take the dot product with $\\mathbf{a}$, we get an expression that is just the cofactor expansion across the first row of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na \\cdot (b \\cdot c) &= (a_{1}i + a_{2}j + a_{3}k) \\cdot (\\begin{vmatrix}b_{2} & b_{3} \\\\ c_{2} & c_{3}\\end{vmatrix}i -\\begin{vmatrix}b_{1} & b_{3} \\\\ c_{1} & c_{3}\\end{vmatrix}j +\\begin{vmatrix}b_{1} & b_{2} \\\\ c_{1} & c_{2}\\end{vmatrix}k) \\\\\n&= a_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}b_{2} & b_{3} \\\\ c_{2} & c_{3}\\end{vmatrix}- a_{2}\\begin{vmatrix}b_{1} & b_{3} \\\\ c_{1} & c_{3}\\end{vmatrix}+ a_{3}\\begin{vmatrix}b_{1} & b_{2} \\\\ c_{1} & c_{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}a_{1} & a_{2} & a_{3} \\\\ b_{1} & b_{2} & b_{3} \\\\ c_{1} & c_{2} & c_{3}\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186107083 1064 "We have been using the fact for three vectors $\\mathbf{a} = \\langle a_1,a_2,a_3 \\rangle,$ $\\mathbf{b} = \\langle b_1,b_2,b_3 \\rangle,$ and $\\mathbf{c} = \\langle c_1,c_2,c_3 \\rangle,$ the scalar triple product can be computed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c)=|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut why is this true? To understand where this result comes from, first notice that in the formula for the triple product, the cross product can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}|k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when we take the dot product with $\\mathbf{a},$ we get an expression that is just the cofactor expansion across the first row of the matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =({a}_{1}i+{a}_{2}j+{a}_{3}k)\\cdot (|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}|k) \\\\  & ={a}_{1}|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|-{a}_{2}|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|+{a}_{3}|\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & {a}_{2} & {a}_{3} \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {c}_{3}\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186107084 1064 "Geometrically, the absolute value of the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})$ represents the volume enclosed by the parallelepiped whose sides are formed by the three vectors $\\mathbf{a}$, $\\mathbf{b}$, and $\\mathbf{c}$.\n\nTo illustrate, consider the parallelepiped shown below. The sides of this parallelepiped are formed by the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$, $\\mathbf{b}$, and $\\mathbf{c}$.\n\n![](images/25/2597b48a010607fca71c551dbce8e9e2bbeda47d0892a582347ae1cdf19f1bf6.png)\n\nThe volume of this parallelepiped is given by the absolute value of the triple product:\n\n$$\nV = \\mid \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) \\mid\n$$\n\nA proof of this result will be given at the end of this lesson." 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186107084 1064 "Geometrically, the absolute value of the scalar triple product $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})$ represents the volume enclosed by the parallelepiped whose sides are formed by the three vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\nTo illustrate, consider the parallelepiped shown below. The sides of this parallelepiped are formed by the vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}.$\n\n![](images/25/2597b48a010607fca71c551dbce8e9e2bbeda47d0892a582347ae1cdf19f1bf6.png)\n\nThe volume of this parallelepiped is given by the absolute value of the triple product:\n\n$$\nV = |\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})|\n$$\n\nA proof of this result will be given at the end of this lesson." 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186107085 1064 "Three vectors are said to be **coplanar** if they are parallel to the same plane.\n\nWhen we have three coplanar vectors, the parallelepiped they span is *flat* on the plane, and therefore its volume is zero. Consequently, the triple product of the vectors is zero as well.\n\nSo, if three vectors are coplanar, then their triple product is zero. The converse is also true: if the triple product of three nonzero vectors is zero, then the vectors are coplanar.\n\nTherefore, we have the following statement:\n\n> $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b}$, and $\\mathbf{c}$ are coplanar $\\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad$ $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = 0$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186107085 1064 "Three vectors are said to be **coplanar** if they are parallel to the same plane.\n\nWhen we have three coplanar vectors, the parallelepiped they span is *flat* on the plane, and therefore its volume is zero. Consequently, the triple product of the vectors is zero as well.\n\nSo, if three vectors are coplanar, then their triple product is zero. The converse is also true: if the triple product of three nonzero vectors is zero, then the vectors are coplanar.\n\nTherefore, we have the following statement:\n\n> $\\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are coplanar $\\quad\\Leftrightarrow\\quad$ $\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) = 0$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186107086 1064 "Let's consider a system of linear equations where the corresponding coefficient matrix is singular. For example, consider the following system and its associated augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y=-5 \\\\ -3x+6y=15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as usual. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 15\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y=-5 \\\\ 0x+0y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation $0x+0y = 0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx - 2y = -5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we describe the **general solution** of our system.\n\n- The first variable in our equation, $x,$ is called a **basic variable**.\n\n- The second variable in our equation, $y,$ is called a **free variable**.\n\nSolving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y),$ we get $x = -5+2y.$ So the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-5+2y \\\\ y=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the free variable $y$ is any number.\n\nIn vector form, this solution is written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5+2y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+y[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that this is the equation of a straight line on a plane. Any point on this line satisfies our system of equations.\n\nIf we want to find some **particular solution** of the system, we simply choose some particular value of $y$ and find the corresponding value of $x$ on the line. For example, if we choose $y=0,$ then the corresponding value is $x=-5$ and the particular solution is $x=-5,$ $y=0.$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186107086 1064 "Let's consider a system of linear equations where the corresponding coefficient matrix is singular. For example, consider the following system and its associated augmented matrix:\n\n$$\nx - 2y =-5; - 3x + 6y = 15\n$$\n\nTo solve the system, we can reduce $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as usual. We have\n\n$$\nM\\begin{vmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 6 & 15\\end{bmatrix} & R_{2}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{2} + 3R_{1}; ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\nx - 2y =-5; 0x + 0y = 0\n$$\n\nSince any pair of numbers $x,y$ is a solution of the final equation\n\n$$\n0x+0y = 0\n$$\n\nwe can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of a single equation\n\n$$\nx - 2y = -5\n$$\n\nFinally, we describe the **general solution** of our system.\n\n- The first variable in our equation, $x$, is called a **basic variable**.\n- The second variable in our equation, $y$, is called a **free variable**.\n\nSolving for the basic variable $(x)$ in terms of the free variable $(y)$, we get\n\n$$\nx = -5+2y\n$$\n\nSo the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx =-5 + 2y; y = y\n$$\n\nwhere the free variable $y$ is any number.\n\nIn vector form, this solution is written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-5 + 2y \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} + y\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, y ∈ (- ∞, ∞)\n$$\n\nNotice that this is the equation of a straight line on a plane. Any point on this line satisfies our system of equations.\n\nIf we want to find some **particular solution** of the system, we simply choose some particular value of $y$ and find the corresponding value of $x$ on the line. For example, if we choose $y=0$, then the corresponding value is $x=-5$ and the particular solution is $x=-5$, $y=0$." 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186107087 1064 "Given a $2 \\times 2$ system of linear equations, we have the following possibilities for the number of solutions:\n\n- The system has exactly *one solution* if and only if the respective coefficient matrix is non-singular (meaning that the determinant is not zero). Such systems are called **consistent independent**.\n\n- The system has *infinitely many solutions* if the second row in the standard echelon form contains only $0$'s. These systems are called **consistent dependent**.\n\n- The system has *no solution* if the second row in the standard echelon form contains only $0$'s on the left-hand side and a non-zero element on the right-hand side. These are called **inconsistent** systems.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\n**Note:** A system of linear equations of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax+by=0 \\\\ cx+dy=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have only $0$'s on the right-hand side, is called **homogeneous**. A homogeneous system always has at least one solution, namely, the solution $x=0$ and $y=0.$ Or, in vector form, $\\langle 0,0 \\rangle.$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186107087 1064 "Given a $2 \\times 2$ system of linear equations, we have the following possibilities for the number of solutions:\n\n- The system has exactly *one solution* if and only if the respective coefficient matrix is non-singular (meaning that the determinant is not zero). Such systems are called **consistent independent**.\n- The system has *infinitely many solutions* if the second row in the standard echelon form contains only $0$'s. These systems are called **consistent dependent**.\n- The system has *no solution* if the second row in the standard echelon form contains only $0$'s on the left-hand side and a non-zero element on the right-hand side. These are called **inconsistent** systems.\n\n![](images/16/1692fbea298339ff839cf2f14986d51af45d1ec72b2b7d0de179336bdef40c0e.png)\n\n**Note:** A system of linear equations of the form\n\n$$\nax + by = 0; cx + dy = 0\n$$\n\nwhere we have only $0$'s on the right-hand side, is called **homogeneous**. A homogeneous system always has at least one solution, namely, the solution $x=0$ and $y=0$. Or, in vector form, $\\langle 0,0 \\rangle$." 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186107090 1064 "We have been using the fact that the volume of a parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{a}$, $\\mathbf{b}$, and $\\mathbf{c}$ is given by the absolute value of the triple product:\n\n$$\nV = \\mid \\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c}) \\mid\n$$\n\nBut why is this formula true? To understand why, remember that the volume of a parallelepiped can be obtained by multiplying the area of the base by the height.\n\nAs we can see in the diagram below,\n\n- the area of the base is given by $A = \\mid \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} \\mid$, and\n- the height is given by $h = \\mid \\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}}\\:\\mathbf{a} \\mid$.\n\n![](images/cf/cfc492127bed6d6929518a708288691c5fe79e1842be6ecf92666bed6b03200b.png)\n\nTo compute the volume, we will multiply the area of the base by the height. First, though, let's write down the expression for the height:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nh &= \\mid proj_{b \\cdot c}a \\mid \\\\\n&= \\mid (a \\cdot (b \\cdot c))/(\\begin{vmatrix}b \\cdot c &)\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (\\begin{vmatrix}a \\cdot (b \\cdot c) &)/(\\mid b \\cdot c\\end{vmatrix})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, multiplying the area of the base by the height, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nV &= A \\cdot h \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}b \\cdot c & \\cdot (\\begin{vmatrix}a \\cdot (b \\cdot c) &)/(\\mid b \\cdot c\\end{vmatrix}) \\\\ = & a \\cdot (b \\cdot c)\\end{vmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186107090 1064 "We have been using the fact that the volume of a parallelepiped spanned by the vectors $\\mathbf{a},$ $\\mathbf{b},$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ is given by the absolute value of the triple product:\n\n$$\nV =  |\\mathbf{a} \\cdot (\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c})|\n$$\n\nBut why is this formula true? To understand why, remember that the volume of a parallelepiped can be obtained by multiplying the area of the base by the height.\n\nAs we can see in the diagram below, \n\n- the area of the base is given by $A = | \\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c} |,$ and\n\n- the height is given by $h = |\\text{proj}_{\\mathbf{b}\\times\\mathbf{c}}\\:\\mathbf{a}|.$\n\n![](images/cf/cfc492127bed6d6929518a708288691c5fe79e1842be6ecf92666bed6b03200b.png)\n\nTo compute the volume, we will multiply the area of the base by the height. First, though, let's write down the expression for the height:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =|{\\text{proj}}_{b\\times c}\\,a| \\\\  & =|\\frac{a\\cdot (b\\times c)}{|b\\times c|}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|a\\cdot (b\\times c)|}{|b\\times c|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, multiplying the area of the base by the height, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =A\\cdot h \\\\  & =|b\\times c|\\cdot \\frac{|a\\cdot (b\\times c)|}{|b\\times c|} \\\\  & =|a\\cdot (b\\times c)|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186107103 1064 "Sometimes, a system of equations might not have any solutions. For example, consider the following augmented matrix:\n\n$$\nM = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is\n\n$$\nx - 2y = 3; 0x + 0y = 10\n$$\n\nThe final equation,\n\n$$\n0x+0y=10\n$$\n\nhas no solution. It simplifies to $0=10$, which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution." 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186107103 1064 "Sometimes, a system of equations might not have any solutions. For example, consider the following augmented matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2y=3 \\\\ 0x+0y=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation, $0x+0y=10,$ has no solution. It simplifies to $0=10,$ which is a false statement.\n\nSo, there is no pair $x,y$ that satisfies both equations in the system. Consequently, the overall system has no solution." 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186107146 1064 "Recall that a random variable $X$ is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A random variable can take on one of many values from a set $S$, called the range or support of $X$.\n\nA **continuous random variable** is a random variable whose set of possible values $S$ is given by an interval of real numbers.\n\nFor example, the height of a randomly selected person could be modeled as a continuous random variable because its possible values form an interval of real numbers. For modeling purposes, we usually assume that the height of a randomly selected person could be *any number* in the real interval $(0,\\infty)$, although we could also restrict this to a finite interval $[a,b]$ if we wanted.\n\nOn the other hand, the result of rolling a $6$-sided die is *not* a continuous random variable because its possible values are $1,2,3,4,5,6$, which does *not* correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\nPhysical quantities, such as mass, length, area, volume, time, fluid concentration, etc, are usually modeled as continuous variables.\n\n![](images/73/73d1a2c3852734a6221654882ad5421adb565c96c718e91283e5fbc1f26a72c7.png)" 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186107146 1064 "Recall that a random variable $X$ is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment. A random variable can take on one of many values from a set $S,$ called the range or support of $X.$\n\nA **continuous random variable** is a random variable whose set of possible values $S$ is given by an interval of real numbers.\n\nFor example, the height of a randomly selected person could be modeled as a continuous random variable because its possible values form an interval of real numbers. For modeling purposes, we usually assume that the height of a randomly selected person could be *any number* in the real interval $(0,\\infty),$ although we could also restrict this to a finite interval $[a,b]$ if we wanted.\n\nOn the other hand, the result of rolling a $6$-sided die is *not* a continuous random variable because its possible values are $1,2,3,4,5,6,$ which does *not* correspond to an interval of real numbers.\n\nPhysical quantities, such as mass, length, area, volume, time, fluid concentration, etc, are usually modeled as continuous variables. \n\n![](images/73/73d1a2c3852734a6221654882ad5421adb565c96c718e91283e5fbc1f26a72c7.png)" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186107147 1064 "The **probability density function** (or **pdf**) of a continuous random variable $X$ defined over a set $S$ is a function $f(x)$ such that the probability that $X$ lies in the interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx\n$$\n\nThus, the probability that $X$ lies between $a$ and $b$ can be interpreted as the area bounded between $f(x)$ and the $x$-axis over the interval $x\\in [a,b]$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e6/e6c21e69a0dbd15ce32d18c2e74d8fcab0276e82bc55b354eaa5140adff610e6.png)\n\nIn general, for a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function over a set $S$, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$ in $S$\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nFor example, let the random variable $X$ be the result of randomly selecting a real number between $0$ and $6$. In this case, the pdf of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\frac{1}{6},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 6, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise\n$$\n\nThis pdf tells us that any number between $0$ and $6$ has an equal chance of being selected, and no number outside this interval can be selected.\n\nNote that this is a valid pdf because it's always nonnegative, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{S}f(x)dx &= \\int_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{6}dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{6}x]_{0}^{6} \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 6] - [\\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 0] \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the probability that our randomly selected number is no greater than $3$, we integrate the pdf over the interval $[0,3]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(0 \\le X \\le 3) &= \\int_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{6}dx \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{6}x]_{0}^{3} \\\\\n&= [\\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 3] - [\\frac{1}{6} \\cdot 0] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result matches our intuition. Since every number has an equal chance of being selected, and $[0,3]$ is half the length of $[0,6]$, we must have\n\n$$\nP(0 \\leq X \\leq 3) = \\dfrac12\n$$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186107147 1064 "The **probability density function** (or **pdf**) of a continuous random variable $X$ defined over a set $S$ is a function $f(x)$ such that the probability that $X$ lies in the interval $[a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$\n\nThus, the probability that $X$ lies between $a$ and $b$ can be interpreted as the area bounded between $f(x)$ and the $x$-axis over the interval $x\\in [a,b],$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e6/e6c21e69a0dbd15ce32d18c2e74d8fcab0276e82bc55b354eaa5140adff610e6.png)\n\nIn general, for a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function over a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nFor example, let the random variable $X$ be the result of randomly selecting a real number between $0$ and $6.$ In this case, the pdf of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 6, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis pdf tells us that any number between $0$ and $6$ has an equal chance of being selected, and no number outside this interval can be selected. \n\nNote that this is a valid pdf because it's always nonnegative, and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{6}\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}x]}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 6]-[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 0] \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the probability that our randomly selected number is no greater than $3,$ we integrate the pdf over the interval $[0,3]{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le X\\le 3) & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}x]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 3]-[\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis result matches our intuition. Since every number has an equal chance of being selected, and $[0,3]$ is half the length of $[0,6],$ we must have $P(0 \\leq X \\leq 3) = \\dfrac12.$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186107148 1064 "The probability density function of a continuous random variable $X$ is somewhat analogous to the probability *mass* function of a discrete random variable in the sense that it describes the probability distribution of $X$.\n\nHowever, unlike the case with discrete random variables, probability density is *not* the same as probability! Instead, we can think of a pdf as a tool for computing probabilities for continuous random variables.\n\nThere are two counterintuitive yet important points to be aware of when it comes to interpreting a pdf:\n\n- *The probability that a continuous random variable takes on any particular value is always equal to $0$.* Continuing the previous example, the probability that our random variable takes on the value $X=5$ is equal to\n$P(X = 5) = P(5 \\leq X \\leq 5) = \\displaystyle \\int_5^5 \\dfrac{1}{6} \\, \\textrm dx = 0$.\nIn general, for any value $a$ and any continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$, we have\n$P(X = a) = P(a \\leq X \\leq a) = \\displaystyle \\int_a^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0$.\n- *The values of a probability density function may be greater than $1$.* For example, let $Y$ be the result of randomly selecting a real number between $0$ and $\\dfrac{1}{2}$. Then, the pdf of $Y$ is given by\n$f(y) = 2, \\qquad y \\in \\left[0, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right]$.\nThis pdf tells us that all numbers between $0$ and $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ are equally likely. Again, $f(y)$ is nonnegative, and\n$\\displaystyle \\int_0^{1/2} f(y) \\, \\textrm d y = \\displaystyle \\int_0^{1/2} 2 \\, \\textrm d y = 1$.\nSo, $f(y)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nLastly, note that since the probability that a continuous random variable takes on a particular value is always equal to $0$, it doesn't matter whether or not we include the endpoints of a particular interval. In other words, for any continuous random variable $X$, we have\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = P(a < X < b) = P(a < X \\leq b) = P(a \\leq X < b) = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx\n$$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186107148 1064 "The probability density function of a continuous random variable $X$ is somewhat analogous to the probability *mass* function of a discrete random variable in the sense that it describes the probability distribution of $X.$ \n\nHowever, unlike the case with discrete random variables, probability density is *not* the same as probability! Instead, we can think of a pdf as a tool for computing probabilities for continuous random variables.  \n\nThere are two counterintuitive yet important points to be aware of when it comes to interpreting a pdf:\n\n- *The probability that a continuous random variable takes on any particular value is always equal to $0.$* \n\nContinuing the previous example, the probability that our random variable takes on the value $X=5$ is equal to\n\n$$\nP(X = 5) = P(5 \\leq X \\leq 5) = \\int_5^5 \\dfrac{1}{6} \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\nIn general, for any value $a$ and any continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X = a) = P(a \\leq X \\leq a) = \\int_a^a f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 0.\n$$\n\n- *The values of a probability density function may be greater than $1.$* \n\nFor example, let $Y$ be the result of randomly selecting a real number between $0$ and $\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ Then, the pdf of $Y$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(y) = 2, \\qquad y \\in \\left[ 0, \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right].\n$$\n\nThis pdf tells us that all numbers between $0$ and $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ are equally likely. Again, $f(y)$ is nonnegative, and \n\n$$\n\\int_0^{1/2} f(y) \\, \\textrm d y = \\int_0^{1/2} 2 \\, \\textrm d y = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(y)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nLastly, note that since the probability that a continuous random variable takes on a particular value is always equal to $0,$ it doesn't matter whether or not we include the endpoints of a particular interval. In other words, for any continuous random variable $X,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = P(a < X < b) = P(a < X \\leq b) = P(a \\leq X < b) = \\int_a^b f(x) \\, \\textrm dx.\n$$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186111882 1064 "When we compute the area between a curve $y=y(x)$ and the $x$-axis using integration, we must bear the following in mind:\n\n- definite integrals that correspond to areas above the $x$-axis are positive,\n- definite integrals that correspond to areas below the $x$-axis are negative.\n\n![](images/db/dbbb9c009908be0bbafc58e68bb1468d9d99f984b3a8087d7ec02d0cbd2ff5db.png)\n\nThe facts above become crucial when we attempt to find the area bounded by a curve that crosses the $x$-axis." 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186111882 1064 "When we compute the area between a curve $y=y(x)$ and the $x$-axis using integration, we must bear the following in mind: \n\n- definite integrals that correspond to areas above the $x$-axis are positive,\n\n- definite integrals that correspond to areas below the $x$-axis are negative.\n\n![](images/db/dbbb9c009908be0bbafc58e68bb1468d9d99f984b3a8087d7ec02d0cbd2ff5db.png)\n\nThe facts above become crucial when we attempt to find the area bounded by a curve that crosses the $x$-axis." 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186111891 1064 "Suppose we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\nx^2\\geq 4\n$$\n\nLet's start by bringing all the terms to the left-hand side, so\n\n$$\nx^{2} - 4 \\mid \\ge 0\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $y = x^2-4$ and find its roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 4 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 2^{2} &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 2)(x + 2) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $x= \\pm2$.\n\nSolving the inequality\n\n$$\nx^2-4 \\geq 0\n$$\n\nis equivalent to finding the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=x^2-4$ is above or on the $x$-axis (where\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}y \\ge 0}\n$$\n\n).\n\nSince the leading coefficient of $x^2-4$ is positive, the parabola opens upward (like $\\smile$). Therefore, the solution set lies outside the roots (and includes the roots), as shown below.\n\n![](images/15/15935c6dd4d6ca124da42ec8bac83ca43a12209b71be5fed1f961e342d1039ac.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the initial inequality is $x \\in {\\color{blue}(-\\infty, -2] \\cup [2, +\\infty)}$." 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186111891 1064 "Suppose we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\nx^2\\geq 4.\n$$\n\nLet's start by bringing all the terms to the left-hand side, so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4 & \\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding parabola given by $y = x^2-4$ and find its roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{2}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the roots are $x= \\pm2.$ \n\nSolving the inequality $x^2-4 \\geq 0$ is equivalent to finding the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y=x^2-4$ is above or on the $x$-axis (where ${\\color{red}y \\ge 0}$). \n\nSince the leading coefficient of $x^2-4$ is positive, the parabola opens upward (like $\\smile$). Therefore, the solution set lies outside the roots (and includes the roots), as shown below.\n\n![](images/15/15935c6dd4d6ca124da42ec8bac83ca43a12209b71be5fed1f961e342d1039ac.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the initial inequality is $x \\in {\\color{blue}(-\\infty, -2] \\cup [2, +\\infty)}.$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186111892 1064 "We can also solve quadratic inequalities by reducing them to absolute value expressions.\n\nFor example, to solve the inequality $x^2 > 1$, we can start by taking the square root of both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\nx^{2}\\begin{vmatrix}> 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x^{2}}\\end{vmatrix}> \\sqrt{1}; \\mid x \\mid \\mid > 1\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of absolute value, we must have $x>1$ or $x<-1$.\n\nSo, the solution is $x \\in (-\\infty, -1) \\cup (1,\\infty)$.\n\n**Remember:** For any number $a$ that is zero or positive,\n\n- the equation $\\mid x \\mid < a$ has the solution $-a < x < a$,\n- the equation $\\mid x \\mid \\leq a$ has the solution $-a \\leq x \\leq a$,\n- the equation $\\mid x \\mid > a$ has the solution $x > a$ or $x < -a$, and\n- the equation $\\mid x \\mid \\geq a$ has the solution $x \\geq a$ or $x \\leq -a$." 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186111892 1064 "We can also solve quadratic inequalities by reducing them to absolute value expressions.\n\nFor example, to solve the inequality $x^2 > 1,$ we can start by taking the square root of both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & >1 \\\\ \\sqrt{{x}^{2}} & >\\sqrt{1} \\\\ |x| & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of absolute value, we must have $x>1$ or $x<-1.$\n\nSo, the solution is $x \\in (-\\infty, -1) \\cup (1,\\infty).$\n\n**Remember:** For any number $a$ that is zero or positive,\n\n- the equation $|x| < a$ has the solution $-a < x < a,$\n\n- the equation $|x| \\leq a$ has the solution $-a \\leq x \\leq a,$\n\n- the equation $|x| > a$ has the solution $x > a$ or $x < -a,$ and\n\n- the equation $|x| \\geq a$ has the solution $x \\geq a$ or $x \\leq -a.$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186111893 1064 "Let's consider the inequality\n\n$$\nx^2 \\geq -4\n$$\n\nWe can solve this equation using either of the two methods that we've learned.\n\n- **Method 1:** Solving an Absolute Value InequalityWe know that the square of *any* real number is greater than or equal to zero. Therefore, the square of any real number is also greater than or equal to $-4$, since $x^2 \\geq 0 > -4$.Therefore, *any* real number will satisfy the inequality $x^2 \\geq -4$. So, we conclude that the solution to the inequality is $x\\in(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\n- **Method 2:** Picturing a Quadratic CurveLet's bring all the terms to the left-hand side,\n$x^2+4 \\geq 0$,\nand then plot the parabola $y = x^2+4$. The desired inequality ($y \\geq 0$) is always satisfied, so the solution is $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$.\n\n![](images/b2/b26de09c0de43abfaf7da81015fcf2b1355859dd1accace02092296bf2415fb3.png)" 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186111893 1064 "Let's consider the inequality $x^2 \\geq -4.$ We can solve this equation using either of the two methods that we've learned.\n\n- **Method 1:** Solving an Absolute Value Inequality\n\nWe know that the square of *any* real number is greater than or equal to zero. Therefore, the square of any real number is also greater than or equal to $-4,$ since $x^2 \\geq 0 > -4.$\n\nTherefore, *any* real number will satisfy the inequality $x^2 \\geq -4.$ So, we conclude that the solution to the inequality is $x\\in(-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- **Method 2:** Picturing a Quadratic Curve\n\nLet's bring all the terms to the left-hand side,\n\n$$\nx^2+4 \\geq 0,\n$$\n\nand then plot the parabola $y = x^2+4.$ The desired inequality ($y \\geq 0$) is always satisfied, so the solution is $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\n![](images/b2/b26de09c0de43abfaf7da81015fcf2b1355859dd1accace02092296bf2415fb3.png)" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186116446 1064 "Many of the known properties of derivatives of scalar functions extend naturally to derivatives of vector functions.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are vector-valued functions, $u(t)$ is a scalar function, and $\\alpha$ and $\\mathbf c$ are scalar and vector constants, respectively.\n\nThe **sum and multiple rules** for differentiating vector-valued functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(f + g)^{′}(t) = f^{′}(t) + g^{′}(t) & sum rule \\\\ (αf)^{′}(t) = αf^{′}(t), (uc)^{′}(t) = u^{′}(t)c & constant multiple rules \\\\ (uf)^{′}(t) = u^{′}(t)f(t) + u(t)f^{′}(t) & scalar function multiple rule\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186116446 1064 "Many of the known properties of derivatives of scalar functions extend naturally to derivatives of vector functions. \n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are vector-valued functions, $u(t)$ is a scalar function, and $\\alpha$ and $\\mathbf c$ are scalar and vector constants, respectively. \n\nThe **sum and multiple rules** for differentiating vector-valued functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f+g)^{'}(t)={f}^{'}(t)+{g}^{'}(t)\\, & \\text{sum rule} \\\\ (\\alpha f)^{'}(t)=\\alpha {f}^{'}(t),\\,(uc)^{'}(t)={u}^{'}(t)c\\, & \\text{constant multiple rules} \\\\ (uf)^{'}(t)={u}^{'}(t)f(t)+u(t){f}^{'}(t)\\, & \\text{scalar function multiple rule}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186116447 1064 "The **chain rule** for differentiating vector-valued functions states that\n\n![](images/57/57f880045af93f552c2736659e377c7ac4c6f41f78a2df446f241783f472f8aa.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/bc/bcf7f1c428a975daf939f469deb8e13c050d912fb5a2548fbb6b60ea6d413a86.svg) is a vector-valued function while ![](images/cd/cd94c3404a0b6460dd11ffac737b2ce09af9f26b21de28ccedf8d028bd6ec38a.svg) is a scalar function." 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186116447 1064 "The **chain rule** for differentiating vector-valued functions states that\n\n$$\n(f(u(t))^{′} = u^{′}(t)f^{′}(u(t))\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{f}(u)$ is a vector-valued function while $u(t)$ is a scalar function." 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186116448 1064 "When working with vector functions, there are two types of products: the dot product and the cross product. So, there are two **product rules** for derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(f\\cdot g)^{'}(t)=f(t)\\cdot {g}^{'}(t)+{f}^{'}(t)\\cdot g(t)\\, & \\text{dot product rule} \\\\ (f\\times g)^{'}(t)={f}^{'}(t)\\times g(t)+f(t)\\times {g}^{'}(t)\\, & \\text{cross product rule}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** Remember that the cross product is anticommutative, meaning that $\\mathbf f \\times \\mathbf g = - \\mathbf g \\times \\mathbf f.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} (\\mathbf f \\times \\mathbf g)(t) = -\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} (\\mathbf g \\times \\mathbf f)(t).\n$$\n\nWhen applying the cross product rule, the order in which the operations are applied is important." 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186116448 1064 "When working with vector functions, there are two types of products: the dot product and the cross product. So, there are two **product rules** for derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}(f\\cdotg)^{′}(t) = f(t)\\cdotg^{′}(t) + f^{′}(t)\\cdotg(t) & dot product rule \\\\ (f \\cdot g)^{′}(t) = f^{′}(t) \\cdot g(t) + f(t) \\cdot g^{′}(t) & cross product rule\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** Remember that the cross product is anticommutative, meaning that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf f \\times \\mathbf g =-\\mathbf g \\times \\mathbf f\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} (\\mathbf f \\times \\mathbf g)(t) = -\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} (\\mathbf g \\times \\mathbf f)(t)\n$$\n\nWhen applying the cross product rule, the order in which the operations are applied is important." 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186116555 1064 "The domain of a vector-valued function $\\mathbf f(t)$ is the set of values of $t$ for which the function is well-defined.\n\nA function is well-defined at some value $t = t_0$ if all its component functions are defined at $t_0$. Thus, the domain of $\\mathbf f$ is the *intersection* of the domains of its component functions.\n\nLet's find the domain of the following function:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t) = \\dfrac{1}{3+t} \\: \\mathbf i + t^2 \\: \\mathbf j + \\sqrt{-t} \\: \\mathbf k\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the domains of the component functions:\n\n- The domain of $\\dfrac{1}{3+t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,-3) \\cup (-3,\\infty)$.\n- The domain of $t^2$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n- The domain of $\\sqrt{-t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty, 0]$.\n\nTo find the domain of $\\mathbf{f}$, we need the intersection of the component functions' domains. This intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7c/7cf7270cc873e9de1fedb7bd413bba1f9ed1f7cd604b3ae640d32d9e0b41bdef.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,-3) \\cup (-3,0]$." 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186116555 1064 "The domain of a vector-valued function $\\mathbf f(t)$ is the set of values of $t$ for which the function is well-defined. \n\nA function is well-defined at some value $t = t_0$ if all its component functions are defined at $t_0.$ Thus, the domain of $\\mathbf f$ is the *intersection* of the domains of its component functions.\n\nLet's find the domain of the following function:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(t) = \\dfrac{1}{3+t} \\: \\mathbf i + t^2 \\: \\mathbf j + \\sqrt{-t} \\: \\mathbf k\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the domains of the component functions:\n\n- The domain of $\\dfrac{1}{3+t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,-3) \\cup (-3,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of $t^2$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\n- The domain of $\\sqrt{-t}$ is $t \\in (-\\infty, 0].$\n\nTo find the domain of $\\mathbf{f},$ we need the intersection of the component functions' domains. This intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/7c/7cf7270cc873e9de1fedb7bd413bba1f9ed1f7cd604b3ae640d32d9e0b41bdef.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function $\\mathbf{f}(t)$ is $t \\in (-\\infty,-3) \\cup (-3,0].$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186121038 1064 "The **real coordinate space** (of dimension $n$) is the set of all possible column vectors of height $n$, whose components are real numbers. Symbolically, we represent the real coordinate space of dimension $n$ as $\\mathbb{R}^n$.\n\nFor example, the following vectors are in $\\mathbb{R}, \\mathbb{R}^2$, $\\mathbb{R}^3$, and $\\mathbb{R}^5$, respectively:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{4} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe've already encountered several operations on vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ or $\\mathbb{R}^3$, namely, vector addition and subtraction, scalar multiplication, and the dot product. All of these operations work the same way in $\\mathbb{R}^n$." 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186121038 1064 "The **real coordinate space** (of dimension $n$) is the set of all possible column vectors of height $n,$ whose components are real numbers. Symbolically, we represent the real coordinate space of dimension $n$ as $\\mathbb{R}^n.$\n\nFor example, the following vectors are in $\\mathbb{R}, \\mathbb{R}^2,$ $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ and $\\mathbb{R}^5,$ respectively: \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe've already encountered several operations on vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ or $\\mathbb{R}^3,$ namely, vector addition and subtraction, scalar multiplication, and the dot product. All of these operations work the same way in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186121039 1064 "In general, if we have two vectors $\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^n$ and a scalar $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb{R}$, then our vector operations are defined as follows:\n\n1. The **sum/difference** of the two vectors is defined as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u_{1} \\\\ u_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ u_{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\n2. **Scalar multiplication** of a vector is defined as\n$\\begin{bmatrix}u_{1} \\\\ u_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ u_{n}\\end{bmatrix}$\n3. The **dot product** of two vectors is defined as\n$\\quad \\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v} = \\underbrace{u_1 \\cdot v_1 + u_2 \\cdot v_2 + \\cdots + u_n \\cdot v_n}_{\\Large{n} \\,\\textrm{-terms}} = \\sum_{i=1}^n u_i v_i$." 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186121039 1064 "In general, if we have two vectors $\\mathbf{u},\\mathbf{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^n$ and a scalar $\\lambda\\in\\mathbb{R},$ then our vector operations are defined as follows:\n\n- The **sum/difference** of the two vectors is defined as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {u}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- **Scalar multiplication** of a vector is defined as\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} \\\\ {u}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {u}_{n}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- The **dot product** of two vectors is defined as\n\n$$\n\\quad \\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v} = \\underbrace{u_1 \\cdot v_1 + u_2 \\cdot v_2 + \\cdots + u_n \\cdot v_n}_{\\Large{n} \\,\\textrm{-terms}} = \\sum_{i=1}^n u_i v_i.\n$$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186121068 1064 "To add two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n$, we just need to add their corresponding components. To illustrate, consider the vectors $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^4$, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b}$, all we have to do is to add each component of the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ with the corresponding component of $\\mathbf{b}$. Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na + b &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5 + (-5) \\\\ 1 + 3 \\\\ -3 + 1 \\\\ 6 + 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can only compute the sum, difference, or dot product of two vectors if both vectors have the same number of components. For example, we can't find the following sum, so we say it's *undefined*:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186121068 1064 "To add two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n,$ we just need to add their corresponding components. To illustrate, consider the vectors $\\mathbf{a},\\mathbf{b} \\in \\mathbb{R}^4,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\mathbf{a} + \\mathbf{b},$ all we have to do is to add each component of the vector $\\mathbf{a}$ with the corresponding component of $\\mathbf{b}.$ Doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5+(-5) \\\\ 1+3 \\\\ -3+1 \\\\ 6+0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can only compute the sum, difference, or dot product of two vectors if both vectors have the same number of components. For example, we can't find the following sum, so we say it's *undefined*:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186125766 1064 "The set of vectors $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\}$ in an abstract vector space $V$ is **linearly dependent** if there exist real scalars $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ not all zero such that \n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{v}_1+x_2\\mathbf{v}_2+\\ldots+x_n\\mathbf{v}_n=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if $x_1 = x_2 = \\cdots = x_n = 0$ is the only solution, the set is **linearly independent**.\n\nLet's consider some examples.\n\n- First, let's consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ of all polynomials in variable $t,$ along with the set\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{ \\underbrace{1}_{\\color{blue}p_1(t)}, \\: \\underbrace{t}_{\\color{blue}p_2(t)}, \\: \\underbrace{t^2}_{\\color{blue}p_3(t)} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $S$ is linearly dependent in $\\mathbb{R}[t],$ we must solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)+{x}_{3}{p}_{3}(t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for $p_1(t),p_2(t)$ and $p_3(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}\\cdot 1+{x}_{2}\\cdot t+{x}_{3}\\cdot {t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}+{x}_{2}t+{x}_{3}{t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0$ is the only solution, $S$ is linearly independent in $\\mathbb R[t].$\n\n- Let's consider the vector space $\\mathbb R[t]$ once more, along with the set \n\n$$\nT = \\big\\{ \\underbrace{t^2+1}_{\\color{red}q_1(t)}, \\: \\underbrace{-2t^2-2}_{\\color{red}q_2(t)} \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo determine whether $T$ is linearly dependent in $\\mathbb{R}[t],$ we must solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}{q}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{q}_{2}(t) & =0 \\\\ {x}_{1}({t}^{2}+1)+{x}_{2}(-2{t}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ ({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2})+({x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ {x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has the nonzero solution $(x_1,x_2)=(2,1).$ As a result, we get\n\n$$\n2 \\cdot {\\color{red}q_1(t)} + 1 \\cdot {\\color{red}q_2(t)} = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the set $\\big\\{ q_1(t), q_2(t) \\big\\}$ is linearly dependent in $\\mathbb R[t].$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186125766 1064 "The set of vectors $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{v}_n \\}$ in an abstract vector space $V$ is **linearly dependent** if there exist real scalars $x_1,x_2,\\ldots,x_n$ not all zero such that\n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{v}_1+x_2\\mathbf{v}_2+\\ldots+x_n\\mathbf{v}_n=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if\n\n$$\nx_1 = x_2 = \\cdots = x_n = 0\n$$\n\nis the only solution, the set is **linearly independent**.\n\nLet's consider some examples.\n\n- First, let's consider the vector space $\\mathbb{R}[t]$ of all polynomials in variable $t$, along with the set\n$S = \\big\\{\\underbrace{1}_{\\color{blue}p_1(t)}, \\: \\underbrace{t}_{\\color{blue}p_2(t)}, \\: \\underbrace{t^2}_{\\color{blue}p_3(t)} \\big\\}$.\nTo determine whether $S$ is linearly dependent in $\\mathbb{R}[t]$, we must solve the equation\n$x_{1}p_{1}(t) + x_{2}p_{2}(t) + x_{3}p_{3}(t)|= 0$.\nSubstituting the given expressions for $p_1(t),p_2(t)$ and $p_3(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n$x_{1} \\cdot 1 + x_{2} \\cdot t + x_{3} \\cdot t^{2}|= 0; x_{1} + x_{2}t + x_{3}t^{2}|= 0$.\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if\n${x_{1} = 0; x_{2} = 0; x_{3} = 0$.\nSince $x_1 = x_2 = x_3 = 0$ is the only solution, $S$ is linearly independent in $\\mathbb R[t]$.\n\n- Let's consider the vector space $\\mathbb R[t]$ once more, along with the set\n$T = \\big\\{\\underbrace{t^2+1}_{\\color{red}q_1(t)}, \\: \\underbrace{-2t^2-2}_{\\color{red}q_2(t)} \\big\\}$.\nTo determine whether $T$ is linearly dependent in $\\mathbb{R}[t]$, we must solve the equation\n$x_{1}q_{1}(t) + x_{2}q_{2}(t)|= 0; x_{1}(t^{2} + 1) + x_{2}(-2t^{2} - 2)|= 0; (x_{1} - 2x_{2}) + (x_{1} - 2x_{2})t^{2}|= 0$.\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if\n${x_{1} - 2x_{2} = 0; x_{1} - 2x_{2} = 0$.\nThis system has the nonzero solution $(x_1,x_2)=(2,1)$. As a result, we get\n$2 \\cdot {\\color{red}q_1(t)} + 1 \\cdot {\\color{red}q_2(t)} = 0$.\nTherefore, the set $\\big\\{q_1(t), q_2(t) \\big\\}$ is linearly dependent in $\\mathbb R[t]$." 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186125767 1064 "Note the following important property of linearly dependent sets.\n\n> *A set of vectors is linearly dependent if and only if it contains a vector that is a linear combination of **other** vectors from that set.*\n\nTo see why this is true, let $\\{ \\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2,\\cdots, \\mathbf{v}_n\\}$ be a linearly dependent set of $n$ vectors. This means that the equation\n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\cdots + x_n\\mathbf{v}_n = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a non-trivial solution, i.e. a solution in which at least one of the coefficients $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is not zero. Without loss of generality, we may assume that $x_1 \\ne 0,$ and we can rewrite the above equation as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1 = -\\dfrac{x_2}{x_1} \\mathbf{v}_2 - \\dfrac{x_3}{x_1}\\mathbf{v}_3 - \\cdots - \\dfrac{x_n}{x_1}\\mathbf{v}_n.\n$$\n\nThe above argument shows that the vector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ can be written as a linear combination of the other vectors from the set." 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186125767 1064 "Note the following important property of linearly dependent sets.\n\n> *A set of vectors is linearly dependent if and only if it contains a vector that is a linear combination of **other** vectors from that set.*\n\nTo see why this is true, let $\\{\\mathbf{v}_1, \\mathbf{v}_2,\\cdots, \\mathbf{v}_n\\}$ be a linearly dependent set of $n$ vectors. This means that the equation\n\n$$\nx_1\\mathbf{v}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{v}_2 + \\cdots + x_n\\mathbf{v}_n = \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a non-trivial solution, i.e. a solution in which at least one of the coefficients $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is not zero. Without loss of generality, we may assume that\n\n$$\nx_1 \\ne 0\n$$\n\nand we can rewrite the above equation as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1 = -\\dfrac{x_2}{x_1} \\mathbf{v}_2 - \\dfrac{x_3}{x_1}\\mathbf{v}_3 - \\cdots - \\dfrac{x_n}{x_1}\\mathbf{v}_n\n$$\n\nThe above argument shows that the vector $\\mathbf{v}_1$ can be written as a linear combination of the other vectors from the set." 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186125796 1064 "One useful property of diagonalization is that it allows us to easily determine the power of a matrix.\n\nAs an example, we consider the $2\\times 2$ matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ with the diagonalization\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe will use the diagonalization of $A$ to compute $A^{55}.$\n\nLet's start by computing $A^2.$ Using the properties of matrix multiplication, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =PD{P}^{-1}\\cdot PD{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =PD\\cancel{{P}^{-1}\\cdot P}D{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P(DD){P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P{D}^{2}{P}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $A^3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{3} & ={A}^{2}\\cdot A \\\\  & =P{D}^{2}{P}^{-1}\\cdot PD{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P{D}^{2}\\cancel{{P}^{-1}\\cdot P}D{P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P({D}^{2}D){P}^{-1} \\\\  & =P{D}^{3}{P}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix $P$ and its inverse $P^{-1}$ will always cancel each other out in the middle of the multiplication! So by induction, we have that\n\n$$\nA^{\\color{blue}55}=P \\cdot D^{\\color{blue}55} \\cdot P^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, we only need to find $D^{\\color{blue}55}.$\n\nSince $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {D}^{3} & ={D}^{2}D=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFollowing this pattern, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{55} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{55}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{55} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{55}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given that $A = PDP^{-1},$ for any non-negative integer ${\\color{blue}k},$ we obtain \n\n$$\nA^{\\color{blue}k} = P \\cdot D^{\\color{blue}k} \\cdot P^{-1}.\n$$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186125796 1064 "One useful property of diagonalization is that it allows us to easily determine the power of a matrix.\n\nAs an example, we consider the $2\\times 2$ matrix $\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$ with the diagonalization\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWe will use the diagonalization of $A$ to compute $A^{55}$.\n\nLet's start by computing $A^2$. Using the properties of matrix multiplication, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{2} &= PDP^{-1} \\cdot PDP^{-1} \\\\\n&= PDP^{-1} \\cdot PDP^{-1} \\\\\n&= P(DD)P^{-1} \\\\\n&= PD^{2}P^{-1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing $A^3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{3} &= A^{2} \\cdot A \\\\\n&= PD^{2}P^{-1} \\cdot PDP^{-1} \\\\\n&= PD^{2}P^{-1} \\cdot PDP^{-1} \\\\\n&= P(D^{2}D)P^{-1} \\\\\n&= PD^{3}P^{-1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the matrix $P$ and its inverse $P^{-1}$ will always cancel each other out in the middle of the multiplication! So by induction, we have that\n\n$$\nA^{\\color{blue}55}=P \\cdot D^{\\color{blue}55} \\cdot P^{-1}\n$$\n\nNow, we only need to find $D^{\\color{blue}55}$.\n\nSince $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nD^{2} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\nD^{3} &= D^{2}D = \\begin{bmatrix}1^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}1^{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFollowing this pattern, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1^{55} & 0 \\\\ 0 & (-1)^{55}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{55} &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}^{55}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{5} & -\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, given that\n\n$$\nA = PDP^{-1}\n$$\n\nfor any non-negative integer ${\\color{blue}k}$, we obtain\n\n$$\nA^{\\color{blue}k} = P \\cdot D^{\\color{blue}k} \\cdot P^{-1}\n$$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186125797 1064 "Remember that the diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ and corresponding linearly independent eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis idea also works in reverse. Given a diagonalization $A$ in the form \n\n$$\nA=PDP^{-1},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe can immediately deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are the diagonal entries of $D,$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of $P.$ This is because the diagonalization of a matrix is (almost) unique.\n\n**Note:** The diagonalization is \"almost\" unique because the order of the eigenvalue-eigenvector pairs may vary, and the eigenvectors may be replaced with scalar multiples of themselves. However, none of these variations change the actual outcome regarding the eigenvalue-eigenvector pairs that we deduce." 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186125797 1064 "Remember that the diagonalization of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ with eigenvalues $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$ and corresponding linearly independent eigenvectors $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ is\n$[\\mid \\mid \\mid; v_{1} \\mid v_{2}; \\mid \\mid \\mid]$\n\nThis idea also works in reverse. Given a diagonalization $A$ in the form\n\n$$\nA=PDP^{-1}\n$$\n\nwe can immediately deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are the diagonal entries of $D$, and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of $P$. This is because the diagonalization of a matrix is (almost) unique.\n\n**Note:** The diagonalization is \"almost\" unique because the order of the eigenvalue-eigenvector pairs may vary, and the eigenvectors may be replaced with scalar multiples of themselves. However, none of these variations change the actual outcome regarding the eigenvalue-eigenvector pairs that we deduce." 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186125845 1064 "A square matrix $A$ is called **diagonalizable** if there exists a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA = PDP^{-1}\n$$\n\nSo, to **diagonalize** a matrix $A$, we need to find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ that satisfy the equation above.\n\nFor example, let's diagonalize the matrix $A$, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo diagonalize a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we follow a three-step **diagonalization algorithm.**\n\n**Step 1:** Find the eigenvalues of the matrix.\n\nWe solve the characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\vert A - \\lambda I \\vert = 0\n$$\n\nFor the given matrix, the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n\\color{red}\\lambda_1 =2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\color{blue}\\lambda_2 =3\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Find a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ consisting of eigenvectors of the matrix.\n\nFollowing the usual method for computing eigenvectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncorresponding to the eigenvalues\n\n$$\n\\color{red}\\lambda_1 =2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\color{blue}\\lambda_2 =3\n$$\n\nrespectively.\n\nNote that\n\n$$\n\\left\\{{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v_1}}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v_2}}\\right\\}\n$$\n\nis a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ since eigenvectors corresponding to distinct eigenvalues are linearly independent. This becomes important later.\n\n**Step 3:** Construct the matrices $D$ and $P$.\n\nNow that we have our eigenvalues and corresponding eigenvectors, we can construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and invertible matrix $P$.\n\nThe matrix $D$ has the eigenvalues of the matrix as its entries on the diagonal. For now, we can write them in any order. So, we let\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThen, the columns of $P$ are the corresponding eigenvectors. We need to write each eigenvector in the *same* column as its respective eigenvalue in the matrix $D$. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA = PDP^{-1} &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can check our solution by computing the product on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} &= \\begin{bmatrix}6 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= A✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186125845 1064 "A square matrix $A$ is called **diagonalizable** if there exists a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA = PDP^{-1}.\n$$\n\nSo, to **diagonalize** a matrix $A,$ we need to find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ that satisfy the equation above.\n\nFor example, let's diagonalize the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo diagonalize a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we follow a three-step **diagonalization algorithm.**\n\n**Step 1:** Find the eigenvalues of the matrix.\n\nWe solve the characteristic equation $\\vert A - \\lambda I \\vert = 0.$ For the given matrix, the eigenvalues are $\\color{red}\\lambda_1 =2$ and $\\color{blue}\\lambda_2 =3.$\n\n**Step 2:** Find a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ consisting of eigenvectors of the matrix.\n\nFollowing the usual method for computing eigenvectors, we get \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ncorresponding to the eigenvalues $\\color{red}\\lambda_1 =2$ and $\\color{blue}\\lambda_2 =3,$ respectively.\n\nNote that $\\left\\{ {\\color{red}\\mathbf{v_1}}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v_2}}\\right\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ since eigenvectors corresponding to distinct eigenvalues are linearly independent. This becomes important later.\n\n**Step 3: ** Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nNow that we have our eigenvalues and corresponding eigenvectors, we can construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and invertible matrix $P.$\n\nThe matrix $D$ has the eigenvalues of the matrix as its entries on the diagonal. For now, we can write them in any order. So, we let\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the columns of $P$ are the corresponding eigenvectors. We need to write each eigenvector in the *same* column as its respective eigenvalue in the matrix $D.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A=PD{P}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can check our solution by computing the product on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186125846 1064 "There is a theorem that states the following:\n\n> *If $A$ is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, then it is diagonalizable if and only if there exists a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ that consists of eigenvectors of $A$.*\n\nWe can break this down into two possible cases:\n\n- If $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues, then it is indeed diagonalizable. This is because two distinct eigenvalues will give rise to two linearly independent eigenvectors (one for each eigenvalue). So, for a matrix $A$ with eigenvalues $\\color{red}\\lambda_1$ and $\\color{blue}\\lambda_2$ and corresponding eigenvectors $\\color{red}\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v_2}$, we have\n$[\\mid \\mid \\mid; v_{1} \\mid v_{2}; \\mid \\mid \\mid]$\n- If we get a double root $\\lambda$ of the characteristic equation, the matrix will be diagonalizable *only if* we can find exactly two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda$, which may or may not be possible. Another way of saying this is that the corresponding eigenspace $V_\\lambda$ must be of dimension $2$." 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186125846 1064 "There is a theorem that states the following:\n\n> *If $A$ is a $2 \\times 2$ matrix, then it is diagonalizable if and only if there exists a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ that consists of eigenvectors of $A.$*\n\nWe can break this down into two possible cases:\n\n- If $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues, then it is indeed diagonalizable. This is because two distinct eigenvalues will give rise to two linearly independent eigenvectors (one for each eigenvalue). \n\nSo, for a matrix $A$ with eigenvalues $\\color{red}\\lambda_1$ and $\\color{blue}\\lambda_2$ and corresponding eigenvectors $\\color{red}\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v_2},$ we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- If we get a double root $\\lambda$ of the characteristic equation, the matrix will be diagonalizable *only if* we can find exactly two linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda,$ which may or may not be possible. Another way of saying this is that the corresponding eigenspace $V_\\lambda$ must be of dimension $2.$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186125847 1064 "If $A$ is a diagonalizable matrix, then we can write\n\n$$\nA=PDP^{-1}\n$$\n\nWe can write this equation in an equivalent form, making $D$ the subject.\n\nFirst, we left-multiply both sides of the equation by $P^{-1}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= PDP^{-1} \\\\\nP^{-1} \\cdot A &= P^{-1} \\cdot PDP^{-1} \\\\\nP^{-1}A &= I_{2}DP^{-1} \\\\\nP^{-1}A &= DP^{-1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we right-multiply both sides of the equation by $P{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP^{-1}A \\cdot P &= DP^{-1} \\cdot P \\\\\nP^{-1}AP &= DI_{2} \\\\\nP^{-1}AP &= D\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA=PDP^{-1} \\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad D = P^{-1}A P\n$$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186125847 1064 "If $A$ is a diagonalizable matrix, then we can write\n\n$$\nA=PDP^{-1}.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can write this equation in an equivalent form, making $D$ the subject.\n\nFirst, we left-multiply both sides of the equation by $P^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =PD{P}^{-1} \\\\ {P}^{-1}\\cdot A & ={P}^{-1}\\cdot PD{P}^{-1} \\\\ {P}^{-1}A & ={I}_{2}D{P}^{-1} \\\\ {P}^{-1}A & =D{P}^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we right-multiply both sides of the equation by $P{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{-1}A\\cdot P & =D{P}^{-1}\\cdot P \\\\ {P}^{-1}AP & =D{I}_{2} \\\\ {P}^{-1}AP & =D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nA=PDP^{-1}\n\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad\nD = P^{-1}A P.\n$$" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186125848 1064 "Given a matrix $A$, the diagonalization\n\n$$\n[ \\mid \\mid \\mid; v_{1} \\mid v_{2}; \\mid \\mid \\mid ]\n$$\n\nis almost unique, but not quite.\n\nWhen we construct the diagonal matrix $D$, we can put the eigenvalues in any order. So this matrix is unique up to permutations of the order of the eigenvalues. Note that by swapping the eigenvalues in $D$, we then have to swap the corresponding eigenvectors (columns) in $P{:}$\n\n$$\n[ \\mid \\mid \\mid; v_{2} \\mid v_{1}; \\mid \\mid \\mid ]\n$$\n\nAlso, since any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is an eigenvector as well, the eigenvector basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ can consist of these multiples instead. So the invertible matrix $P$ is unique up to multiples of its columns." 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186125848 1064 "Given a matrix $A,$ the diagonalization \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & {v}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis almost unique, but not quite.\n\nWhen we construct the diagonal matrix $D,$ we can put the eigenvalues in any order. So this matrix is unique up to permutations of the order of the eigenvalues. Note that by swapping the eigenvalues in $D,$ we then have to swap the corresponding eigenvectors (columns) in $P{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ {v}_{2} & {v}_{1} \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAlso, since any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is an eigenvector as well, the eigenvector basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ can consist of these multiples instead. So the invertible matrix $P$ is unique up to multiples of its columns." 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186125902 1064 "Let ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) be a diagonalizable ![](images/25/2521651122403a440e682fef383af333958f5b0d1334abe8b0771865d4daf100.svg) matrix. Then we can write\n![](images/d8/d8f75220bef8327a8dfc49a6ae22e79c6f1649aefdc8fb43065e0616975a8c5c.svg)\nwhere\n\n- ![](images/1a/1aaddf0d4be4d873ff6c0600b0b239e0565a05ca8daaff7ca92bd5c70bebcf03.svg) is an invertible matrix whose columns are eigenvectors ![](images/13/13cfebc7f22b3c6cfe7a66492040f9c7682e27e72b0a9adbc033e6a815c68fd9.svg) and ![](images/d0/d0c6e1eed1577bd5d71e38657d494bf53798b9c1f52c54e4a270e200df1cc351.svg) of ![](images/c2/c2c85081d1f89d74b8ebb2761496d211d7052193809b7864e7983437f5d0b80f.svg) and\n\n- ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) is a diagonal matrix whose diagonal entries are the corresponding eigenvalues ![](images/ff/ff5243fdeda78658220d1bcd4ac9b5963157b5e1f8b6a31231c68a6743248b19.svg) and ![](images/4a/4a2dd44cea07e06ccec03fff120c7fdbcebe19ad7a31d8abde1ab95c8a6b53a1.svg)\n\nTo understand this better, we need to distinguish between two coordinate systems.\n\nIn ![](images/fb/fb3130e489fdca80d1a077702861c20532fe613f9942d48fa1ec24055494a383.svg) the **standard basis** is\n![](images/d2/d29d3ee4ae42a8d4ee5304cb0d29c6c811838d7b893a3a831b1b1b8b3af12561.svg)\n\nSo, if a vector is written as\n![](images/bb/bbbc0379f1691a30dec4c34ec6171db1f116bec12a24891169957af68164f8d3.svg)\nthen ![](images/12/12adb8ba7ed45d89c9d9a80eb7c06616c41999e9fac57732bc24d2b55e1f8073.svg) The numbers ![](images/34/349015631ec44ef653936c85586d2c5ef56ba64392b3f92b4b92cbef3e731bc5.svg) and ![](images/a7/a77a353782459b753313d80235712695d65dc4a44d8d357aa41add6729f81d8f.svg) are the *standard coordinates* of ![](images/1f/1f3d1a60926323e963d7563529669e5cf8210771c8cbb0379f7a7d90e7c6aeec.svg)\n\nNow let\n![](images/58/58cdfb66e4c93d643fb97273610b3bbf67c8831516d4ccfc5b7790d55a169b28.svg)\n\nSince the columns of ![](images/1a/1aaddf0d4be4d873ff6c0600b0b239e0565a05ca8daaff7ca92bd5c70bebcf03.svg) are ![](images/13/13cfebc7f22b3c6cfe7a66492040f9c7682e27e72b0a9adbc033e6a815c68fd9.svg) and ![](images/d0/d0c6e1eed1577bd5d71e38657d494bf53798b9c1f52c54e4a270e200df1cc351.svg) written in standard coordinates, the matrix ![](images/1a/1aaddf0d4be4d873ff6c0600b0b239e0565a05ca8daaff7ca92bd5c70bebcf03.svg) converts coordinates from the basis ![](images/be/be2d796b77bebb31c0c06cef6af82bc9fb606a584a52cc5385cd32c4e143236c.svg) to the standard basis.\n\nSo, for any vector ![](images/b2/b2cb236f7a7f426f303b1391f76900b47f7d2fa1ad9c8110d8584182559017fd.svg)\n![](images/7e/7e5a6a4b9bee9550e68c75a3945ad25d41f1de6dd3b089ce89005428da169f9d.svg)\n\nHere, ![](images/4f/4fa2300af121b336bb0d7845b8e2b3e34be5a517a1bca45b4ec7b6514b2c544d.svg) denotes the standard-coordinate column of the vector, while ![](images/3f/3f7132e53e5a27bee4c84bdddbac07971ec44ce12539c0e0c23bf0006057e199.svg) denotes the coordinate column of the same vector in the eigenvector basis ![](images/8d/8d16f7d70344ddea7a4fe48105e5ea75b984f48fd77f6f6eb89d26ad91b65805.svg)\n\nAlso, in the basis ![](images/da/dab6f1afbeb705eef0eaf1a338c977e9cf6578010ebf95a6fccd1a4a4610dcd4.svg) the eigenvectors have especially simple coordinate columns:\n![](images/9f/9f5fec87a4253a3a1741c3380e2c6f9331928883ab2083dc02bf0e8efda6a98a.svg)\n\nThis is because\n![](images/30/306d2bb3211dac2883b1eb6abaed54259272a8bcc6a4e562ff5da6fc52dee520.svg)\n\nSo, in the basis ![](images/da/dab6f1afbeb705eef0eaf1a338c977e9cf6578010ebf95a6fccd1a4a4610dcd4.svg) the matrix ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) acts by scaling the first coordinate by ![](images/ff/ff5243fdeda78658220d1bcd4ac9b5963157b5e1f8b6a31231c68a6743248b19.svg) and the second coordinate by ![](images/4a/4a2dd44cea07e06ccec03fff120c7fdbcebe19ad7a31d8abde1ab95c8a6b53a1.svg)\n\nWe can summarize this process for any vector ![](images/4f/4fa2300af121b336bb0d7845b8e2b3e34be5a517a1bca45b4ec7b6514b2c544d.svg) as a sequence of three steps:\n\n- first, rewrite ![](images/4f/4fa2300af121b336bb0d7845b8e2b3e34be5a517a1bca45b4ec7b6514b2c544d.svg) in the eigenvector basis ![](images/da/dab6f1afbeb705eef0eaf1a338c977e9cf6578010ebf95a6fccd1a4a4610dcd4.svg)\n\n- then, apply the diagonal matrix ![](images/59/5914524b1d3db63c66998b75ca1d00a5dc4e294d2a8f10fe384d2b2ebf2af38e.svg) in that basis,\n\n- finally, convert the result back to standard coordinates.\n\nThe diagram below shows this sequence.\n\n![](images/14/1459b0643f99c186f9710118617f89f34a601526e7154213471404d2feb2aaeb.svg)" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186125902 1064 "Let $A$ be a diagonalizable $2 \\times 2$ matrix. Therefore, we can write the matrix $A$ as\n\n$$\nA = P D P^{-1}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $P$ is an invertible $2 \\times 2$ matrix whose columns are the *unit* eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$ corresponding to eigenvalues ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2}$, and\n- $D$ is a diagonal matrix with ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2}$ on the main diagonal.\n\nNow, suppose that $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^2$. Then, the image of $\\mathbf x$ under the action of $A$ is the same as that of $\\mathbf x$ under the action of the sequence $P^{-1} \\to D \\to P$. In fact, *every* vector in the plane is mapped to a new vector under this sequence.\n\nLet's consider the effect of these equivalent actions on a unit circle, with the two unit eigenvectors (not necessarily orthogonal) forming a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\n![](images/dd/dd1a38e4d4b8f96b1c0f9175fbecd133574a0711abc825cb832793718fdee4d3.png)\n\nNotice that multiplying $P$ on the right by the first and second vectors of the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$, we obtain the vectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$, respectively. Thus, $P$ represents a linear transformation of the plane that maps the standard basis $\\{{\\color{red}\\mathbf{e}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{e}_2} \\}$ onto our basis of eigenvectors $\\{{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\}$.\n\nWe can now describe the matrix decomposition $PDP^{-1}$ geometrically, as follows:\n\n- The matrix $P^{-1}$ maps $\\{{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\}$ onto the standard basis $\\{{\\color{red}\\mathbf{e}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{e}_2} \\}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n- The diagonal matrix $D$ represents a scaling along the $x$- and $y$-axes by ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2}$, respectively.\n- Finally, the matrix $P$ maps us back to the original space.\n\nNote that since the eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$ are not orthogonal in general, the matrix $P^{-1}$ usually distorts the space. Therefore, the image of the unit circle under the action of $P^{-1}$ is not necessarily a circle.\n\nThis also can be interpreted in terms of changing the coordinates.\n\nSince the columns of $P$ are the eigenvectors given in the standard basis, we have that $P$ is the change-of-coordinate matrix from the basis of eigenvectors $\\{{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\}$ to the standard basis $\\{{\\color{red}\\mathbf{e}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{e}_2} \\}$. Thus, its inverse, the matrix $P^{-1}$, is the change-of-coordinate matrix from the standard basis to our basis of eigenvectors.\n\nWe can now describe the matrix decomposition $PDP^{-1}$ in terms of changing the coordinates, as follows:\n\n- The matrix $P^{-1}$ changes the coordinates of any vector of the plane from the standard basis $\\{{\\color{red}\\mathbf{e}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{e}_2} \\}$ to the basis of eigenvectors $\\{{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}, {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} \\}$.\n- The diagonal matrix $D$ scales the $1$st and $2$nd coordinates by ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2}$, respectively.\n- Finally, the matrix $P$ changes the coordinates of the result back to the standard basis." 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186125902 1067 "Diagonalization as a Change of Coordinates" 13194139533437 17592186045585 true] [17592186125902 1067 "Geometrical Interpretation of Matrix Diagonalization" 13194139533437 17592186045585 false] [17592186